# Springs-only rotamer/hinge mechanism (ubiquitin, 1D3Z)

Instead of a distance cutoff, the spring network is built directly from
`data/reducerules/ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody` (`pdb2spn --rigidbody`): every
pairwise spring within a named rigid-body group (CA tetrahedron, peptide
plane, chi1..4 pivots, aromatic/imidazole rings, guanidinium, hydroxyl/thiol
rotations), and nothing else. Backbone phi/psi and every side-chain chi
remain genuine, free rotational degrees of freedom -- not frozen the way a
plain cutoff-based elastic network would freeze them.

Ubiquitin (NMR, all-atom + hydrogens) was picked for amino-acid variety: 18
of the 20 standard residues are present (only Cys and Trp are missing),
including a real Pro (whole side chain treated as one rigid ring closing
back onto the backbone N, no free chi) and both a free N-terminus and
C-terminus.

This example needs a local BioSpring build with `--rigidbody` and
`data/reducerules/ProteinAtomRigidGroups.rbody` -- see the Makefile.

### Build and visualize

    make prep
    make vis   (VMD: ball-and-stick + spring-network overlay)

### Launch the simulation

    make run / make run_now
